-                    <p><em>The DeviceAlert state machine showing transitions, and the interplay between alert conditions and signals.</em></p>
+                    <p><img src="devicealert-state-machine.svg" alt="The DeviceAlert state machine showing relationship between the DeviceAlert status and signal annuciation." style="max-width: 100%; height: auto;"/></p> 
+                    <p><em>The compound DeviceAlert state machine covering condition and signal states with common transitions.</em></p>
                 </div>
-                <p>The center row of the diagram shows the flow between the states of the device alert record taking into account both the alert condition and the signals being emitted by the device. The upper row shows the values of key attributes of the DeviceAlert resource instance and of the associated DeviceMetric or Device in the corresponding states. Note that the alert detection activation state of the associated DeviceMetric or Device is represented through a resource extension, more specifically through the `activationState` sub-extension of the <a href="http://hl7.org/fhir/StructureDefinition/device-alertDetection">Device Alert Detection</a> extension, which could be applied to either Device or DeviceMetric. The bottom row shows values of key attributes of the DeviceAlert resource instance in the corresponding states.</p>
+                <p>Notes:
+                        <ol>
+                                <li>An alert condition becoming active is a trigger to start signaling.</li>
+                                <li>The signal state is composed of two attributes: <code>signal.activationState</code> and <code>signal.presence</code>. A single alert signal as identified by <code>signal.identifier</code> may have multiple signal state segments over time.</li>
+                        </ol>
+                </p>
+                <p>A DeviceAlert is created when a device detects a specific, often safety-relevant, condition and records that condition as a distinct alert instance. While the triggering condition is present, the DeviceAlert represents both the alert condition and its signaling state, reflecting whether and how the device is actively notifying users. The resource may be updated as the alert evolves, for example if its priority changes or signaling is modified, but it does not model downstream clinical actions. When the triggering condition is no longer present or no longer considered relevant, the DeviceAlert is deactivated, marking the end of the alert lifecycle. The resource is retained as a historical record to support documentation, review, and analysis of device alerting behavior.</p>
+                <p>A signal associated with a DeviceAlert begins when the device initiates annunciation of an active alert condition, represented by the creation of <code>signal</code> element with a unique <code>signal.identifier</code>, <code>signal.activationState = on</code> and <code>signal.presence = on</code>. The signal may evolve through multiple states, such as being paused (<code>signal.activationState = paused</code>) when temporarily suppressed, acknowledged (<code>signal.presence = ack</code>) when user action stops annunciation while the condition may persist, or modified (e.g., annunciator changes). Each state change can be captured as a distinct signal element with the same <code>signal.identifier</code>. When the alert condition ends (<code>occurrence.end</code> is set), the signal typically transitions to off, but may instead enter a latched state (<code>signal.presence = latched</code>) where annunciation persists beyond the condition to ensure awareness, before finally stopping. Throughout its lifecycle, the signal is thus a dynamic, time-segmented representation of how the device communicates the alert condition, independent of but driven by the underlying condition lifecycle.</p>
         </div>
 </div>
